# jq engine import

This directory contains the jq engine snapshot from commit
`a829f678f340da83a75fb35457d918c98524fdc7`.

Boundary:

- `src/vendor/jq/` owns imported jq engine code and generated parser sources.
- `src/lib/jq/` owns bx runtime adapters used by the engine.
- `src/applets/text/jq/` owns CLI parsing, compatibility defaults, help,
  diagnostics, and exit policy.

The imported jq `main.c` is intentionally absent. bx links the engine and
adapter libraries directly into the multicall executable and enters through
`bx_jq_main()` and `bx_jq_cli_main()`. There is no external jq process and no
preprocessor renaming of `main`.

Local engine patches are pinned by the jq allocation, JSON-parser, filter
recursion, and regex policy audits in `bx-tests`.

## Updating

From the `bx` repository:

```sh
./tools/update-jq
```

That resolves jq's current upstream HEAD to an exact commit, constructs the
engine and complete test-corpus candidates privately, applies the checked-in
patch series, regenerates metadata, then builds bx and runs every jq test
before committing the update. Any failure restores the prior engine, tests,
and metadata.

When the sibling `../bx-tests` repository exists, the command also imports
every tracked upstream jq test there, regenerates its corpus manifest, and
runs the full jq matrix. Without that repository, the same command remains
self-contained: it runs the fetched upstream corpus directly against the
candidate `bx` binary.

To reproduce or select a specific revision:

```sh
./tools/update-jq a829f678f340da83a75fb35457d918c98524fdc7
```

To test whether the latest source and patch series are compatible without
changing either repository:

```sh
./tools/update-jq --check
```
